module.exports = {
  id: 'prometheus', title: 'Prometheus from Scratch', sub: 'Metrics, PromQL, exporters, alerts and Kubernetes monitoring', cat: 'Observability',
  accent: '#e6522c', icon: 'chart', target: 32, keyword: 'PROMQL', tags: '#prometheus #monitoring #sre',
  scenes: [
    { type: 'hook', title: 'Prometheus from Scratch', kicker: 'Monitoring · 32 minutes', q: 'Why do you almost always wrap counters in rate()?',
      n: 'Logs tell you what happened. Metrics tell you how much, how often, and how fast, all the time. Prometheus, now on version three, is the open-source standard for metrics, and the second project ever to graduate from the CNCF. In ten minutes, we run it, write PromQL, and set up alerts, and you will learn why you almost always wrap a counter in rate.' },
    { type: 'roadmap', n: 'Here is the plan: how Prometheus stores and scrapes data, designing good metrics, PromQL in depth, recording rules and SLO burn-rate alerts, Alertmanager routing and unit tests for your alerts, monitoring Kubernetes with the Prometheus Operator, scaling with cardinality control, remote write and long term storage, and what changed in Prometheus three point zero to three point sixteen.' },
    { type: 'step', x: 1, chip: 'Analogy', title: 'A nurse doing rounds', icon: 'heart',
      items: [['clock', 'Every 15 seconds, visit each patient', 'The scrape interval'], ['gauge', 'Read the monitors', 'The /metrics endpoint'], ['file', 'Write it on the chart', 'The time series database'], ['bell', 'Call the doctor on danger', 'Alertmanager']],
      n: 'Picture a nurse doing rounds. Every fifteen seconds, she visits each patient, reads the monitors, and writes the numbers on a chart. If something crosses a danger line, she calls the doctor. That is Prometheus: scrape, store, and alert.' },

    { sec: 'Foundations', short: 'How it works', title: 'How Prometheus works',
      nodes: [['server', 'Targets', 'expose /metrics'], ['chart', 'Prometheus', 'scrapes + stores'], ['bell', 'Alertmanager', 'routes alerts'], ['dash', 'Grafana', 'dashboards']], edges: ['pull, every 15s', 'firing alerts', 'PromQL'],
      tip: 'Prometheus pulls metrics. Targets just serve a page of numbers.',
      n: 'Step one: how it works. Targets expose a slash metrics page of plain text numbers. Prometheus pulls, or scrapes, that page on an interval, and stores the samples in its time series database. Alerts go to Alertmanager, and Grafana draws dashboards with PromQL.' },
    { sec: 'Foundations', short: 'Data model', title: 'The data model: time series and labels',
      cmd: ['# metric name{labels} value', 'http_requests_total{service="api",method="GET",code="200"} 18342', 'http_requests_total{service="api",method="GET",code="500"} 27', 'node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes{instance="web-01:9100"} 4.12e+09'],
      tip: 'Every unique set of labels is its own time series.',
      n: 'Step two: the data model. Every sample has a metric name, a set of labels, and a value, stored with a timestamp. Labels, like service, method, and status code, let you slice the data any way you want. Each unique label combination is its own time series.' },
    { sec: 'Internals', title: 'The scrape cycle, end to end', nodes: [['search', 'Discover', 'service discovery'], ['filter', 'Relabel', 'keep, drop, rewrite'], ['download', 'Scrape', 'HTTP GET /metrics'], ['database', 'Store', 'TSDB head + WAL'], ['bell', 'Evaluate', 'rules every interval']], edges: ['targets', 'labels', 'samples', ''],
      tip: 'Every scrape also writes up, scrape_duration_seconds and scrape_samples_scraped. Use them.',
      n: 'Step three: the scrape cycle. Service discovery finds targets. Relabelling decides which to keep and what labels they carry. Prometheus then pulls the metrics page over HTTP, and stores the samples in its time series database. Rules are evaluated every interval. Each scrape also records its own health: up is one or zero, plus the duration and sample count, and those are the first things to alert on.' },
    { sec: 'Internals', title: 'How the TSDB stores data', cols: ['Part', 'What it is', 'Why it matters'],
      rows: [['Head block', 'Last ~2 hours in memory', 'Fast queries; memory scales with series'], ['WAL', 'Write-ahead log on disk', 'Recover the head after a crash'], ['Blocks', '2h blocks compacted to larger', 'Immutable, indexed, mmap-ed'], ['Retention', 'By time and/or size', '--storage.tsdb.retention.time / .size'], ['Compression', '~1.3 bytes per sample', 'Capacity = series x samples x bytes']],
      tip: 'Plan memory for active series, not for samples. Roughly 3 to 8 KB per active series is a safe estimate.',
      n: 'Step four: how the database stores data. The head block holds roughly the last two hours in memory, backed by a write ahead log for crash recovery. Older data is compacted into immutable, indexed blocks on disk. Retention is set by time or by size with two flags. Samples compress to around one point three bytes each, so disk is cheap; memory scales with the number of active series, which is the number to plan for.' },
    { sec: 'Internals', title: 'Staleness, up, and what "no data" means', items: [['x', 'A target disappears', 'Series get a stale marker; queries stop returning them'], ['check', 'up == 0', 'Scrape failed: target down or unreachable'], ['search', 'absent(metric)', 'Alert when a series should exist and does not'], ['clock', 'Lookback delta: 5 minutes', 'Instant queries look back this far for a sample']],
      cmd: ['up{job="api"} == 0', 'absent(up{job="api"})', 'absent_over_time(up{job="api"}[10m])'],
      tip: 'up == 0 cannot fire if the target was never discovered. Pair it with absent() for critical jobs.',
      n: 'Step five: staleness. When a target disappears, Prometheus writes a stale marker so the series stops appearing immediately. Up equals zero means a scrape failed. But if a target was never discovered, up equals zero cannot fire, so for critical jobs add an absent alert too. Instant queries look back five minutes for the latest sample.' },
    { sec: 'Foundations', short: 'Metric types', title: 'The four metric types', cols: ['Behaviour', 'Example'],
      rows: [['Counter', 'Only goes up (resets on restart)', 'http_requests_total'], ['Gauge', 'Goes up and down', 'memory in use, queue length'], ['Histogram', 'Counts values in buckets', 'request duration buckets'], ['Summary', 'Pre-computed quantiles', 'client-side p99']],
      n: 'Step six: four metric types. A counter only ever goes up, like total requests. A gauge goes up and down, like memory in use. A histogram counts values into buckets, like request durations. A summary computes quantiles in the client, but it cannot be combined across instances.' },
    { sec: 'Foundations', short: 'Run Prometheus', title: 'Run Prometheus',
      cmd: ['# prometheus.yml', 'global: { scrape_interval: 15s }', 'scrape_configs:', '  - job_name: prometheus', '    static_configs: [{ targets: ["localhost:9090"] }]', '$ docker run -d -p 9090:9090 -v "$PWD/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml" prom/prometheus', '# open http://localhost:9090/targets  →  prometheus  UP   (Prometheus 3.x UI)'],
      n: 'Step seven: run it. A minimal config sets the scrape interval, and one job that scrapes Prometheus itself. Start it with Docker, open the targets page, and you will see the job marked up. You are already collecting hundreds of metrics.' },

    { sec: 'Collect', short: 'Exporters', title: 'Exporters: metrics for anything',
      items: [['server', 'node_exporter', 'CPU, memory, disk, network'], ['globe', 'blackbox_exporter', 'Probe HTTP, TCP, DNS, ICMP'], ['database', 'mysqld / postgres exporters', 'Database internals'], ['layers', 'Hundreds more', 'Redis, Kafka, nginx, JMX…']],
      cmd: ['$ docker run -d --net=host --pid=host -v /:/host:ro prom/node-exporter --path.rootfs=/host', '$ curl -s localhost:9100/metrics | grep ^node_load1', 'node_load1 0.42'],
      n: 'Step eight: exporters translate other systems into Prometheus metrics. node exporter covers a machine\'s CPU, memory, disks, and network. blackbox exporter probes endpoints from the outside. And there are exporters for databases, Redis, Kafka, and hundreds more.' },
    { sec: 'Collect', short: 'Instrument your app', title: 'Instrument your own app',
      cmd: ['from prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, start_http_server', 'REQS = Counter("http_requests_total", "Requests", ["method", "code"])', 'LAT = Histogram("http_request_duration_seconds", "Latency")', 'start_http_server(8000)', '@LAT.time()', 'def handle(req):', '    REQS.labels(req.method, "200").inc()'],
      tip: 'Official client libraries exist for Go, Java, Python, Ruby, .NET and more.',
      n: 'Step nine: instrument your own app with a client library. Define a counter for requests, labelled by method and status code, and a histogram for latency. Start the metrics server, increment the counter for each request, and time each handler.' },
    { sec: 'Metric design', title: 'Name and label metrics well', cols: ['Do', 'Avoid'],
      rows: [['http_request_duration_seconds', 'requestLatencyMs (wrong unit, wrong style)'], ['Base units: seconds, bytes, ratio', 'Milliseconds, KB, percent'], ['_total suffix on counters', 'Counters without _total'], ['Few, bounded label values', 'user_id, email, URL path, trace id'], ['One metric, many label values', 'Metric name per endpoint']],
      tip: 'Check with promtool check metrics. Every label multiplies the number of time series.',
      n: 'Step ten: name metrics well. Use base units, seconds and bytes, a total suffix for counters, and snake case. Use labels for dimensions, but only with a small, bounded set of values: never user I Ds, email addresses, raw U R L paths, or trace I Ds, because every distinct value creates a new time series. Promtool check metrics lints names and types for you.' },
    { sec: 'Metric design', title: 'Instrument a service: the RED and USE methods', cols: ['Method', 'For', 'Measure'],
      rows: [['RED', 'Request-driven services', 'Rate, Errors, Duration'], ['USE', 'Resources (CPU, disk, queue)', 'Utilization, Saturation, Errors'], ['Four golden signals', 'Any user-facing system', 'Latency, traffic, errors, saturation']],
      cmd: ['from prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, start_http_server', 'REQS = Counter("http_requests", "Requests", ["method", "code"])', 'LAT  = Histogram("http_request_duration_seconds", "Latency", ["route"],', '                 buckets=(0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2.5, 5))', 'with LAT.labels(route="/orders").time(): handle()', 'REQS.labels("GET", "200").inc()', 'start_http_server(8000)'],
      n: 'Step eleven: what to measure. For services, the RED method: request rate, error rate, and duration. For resources like CPU, disks, and queues, the U S E method: utilization, saturation, and errors. This Python example adds a counter for requests by method and code, and a histogram for latency by route, with buckets chosen around your latency target.' },
    { sec: 'Metric design', title: 'Classic vs native histograms', cols: ['', 'Classic histogram', 'Native histogram'],
      rows: [['Buckets', 'Fixed, you choose', 'Exponential, automatic'], ['Series per histogram', 'One per bucket + sum + count', 'One series'], ['Resolution', 'Only what you configured', 'High, adapts to the data'], ['Aggregation across pods', 'Same buckets required', 'Always compatible'], ['Status', 'Stable', 'Stable since 3.9 (opt in per scrape)']],
      cmd: ['global:', '  scrape_native_histograms: true', 'scrape_configs:', '  - job_name: app', '    scrape_native_histograms: true', '    scrape_protocols: [PrometheusProto, OpenMetricsText1.0.0, PrometheusText0.0.4]', '# query: no _bucket and no le label', 'histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (job) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds[5m])))'],
      tip: 'Native histograms need a client library that exposes them. Opt in per scrape config.',
      n: 'Step twelve: native histograms. Classic histograms need fixed buckets and create one series per bucket. Native histograms use automatic exponential buckets in a single series, with higher resolution, and always aggregate across pods. They became stable in Prometheus three point nine, and you opt in with scrape native histograms. The query drops the bucket suffix and the le label.' },
    { sec: 'Collect', short: 'Service discovery', title: 'Service discovery and relabelling',
      cmd: ['scrape_configs:', '  - job_name: kubernetes-pods', '    kubernetes_sd_configs: [{ role: pod }]', '    relabel_configs:', '      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_annotation_prometheus_io_scrape]', '        action: keep', '        regex: "true"', '      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_namespace]', '        target_label: namespace'],
      n: 'Step thirteen: in dynamic environments, targets come and go. Service discovery finds them automatically, from Kubernetes, AWS, Consul, and more. Relabelling then keeps only the targets you want, and copies useful metadata, like the namespace, into labels.' },
    { sec: 'Collect deep', title: 'Relabelling, the actions that matter',
      cmd: ['relabel_configs:                                  # before the scrape: choose and shape targets', '  - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_annotation_prometheus_io_scrape]', '    action: keep', '    regex: "true"', '  - source_labels: [__address__, __meta_kubernetes_pod_annotation_prometheus_io_port]', '    action: replace', '    regex: ([^:]+)(?::\\d+)?;(\\d+)', '    replacement: $1:$2', '    target_label: __address__', '  - action: labeldrop', '    regex: pod_template_hash', 'metric_relabel_configs:                           # after the scrape: filter samples', '  - source_labels: [__name__]', '    regex: "go_gc_.*"', '    action: drop'],
      tip: 'relabel_configs act on targets. metric_relabel_configs act on samples. Both validated with promtool check config.',
      n: 'Step fourteen: relabelling. Relabel configs run before the scrape, to keep only annotated pods, to build the address from the pod I P and port annotation, and to drop noisy labels. Metric relabel configs run after the scrape and filter individual samples, for example dropping Go garbage collection metrics you never use. This is your main tool for controlling cardinality and cost.' },
    { sec: 'Collect deep', title: 'Probing from outside: blackbox exporter', cmd: ['- job_name: blackbox', '  metrics_path: /probe', '  params: { module: [http_2xx] }', '  static_configs: [{ targets: ["https://shop.example.com"] }]', '  relabel_configs:', '    - { source_labels: [__address__], target_label: __param_target }', '    - { source_labels: [__param_target], target_label: instance }', '    - { target_label: __address__, replacement: "blackbox:9115" }', 'probe_success == 0', 'probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry - time() < 14 * 86400'],
      tip: 'Alert on certificate expiry 14 days ahead. Probe from outside your network, too.',
      n: 'Step fifteen: probing from outside. The blackbox exporter checks H T T P, TCP, DNS, and ICMP endpoints as a user would. Relabelling passes each target to the exporter as a parameter. Probe success equal to zero means the endpoint is failing, and the certificate expiry metric lets you alert fourteen days before a certificate lapses, which prevents a classic outage.' },

    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'Selectors', title: 'PromQL: selectors and ranges',
      cmd: ['http_requests_total{service="api", code=~"5.."}', '# instant vector: the latest value per series', 'http_requests_total{service="api"}[5m]', '# range vector: every sample from the last 5 minutes', 'up == 0', '# targets that are down right now'],
      tip: '= equals · != not · =~ regex · !~ not regex',
      n: 'Step sixteen: PromQL basics. A selector picks series by name and labels, and equals tilde matches a regular expression, like any five hundred code. Add a duration in square brackets to get a range of samples. And up equals zero lists every target that is down right now.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'rate()', title: 'rate(): turn counters into speeds',
      cmd: ['rate(http_requests_total{service="api"}[5m])', '{code="200", method="GET"}  61.2   # requests per second', '{code="500", method="GET"}   0.09', 'increase(http_requests_total[1h])', '# total requests in the last hour'],
      tip: 'A raw counter only grows. rate() gives per-second speed and handles restarts.',
      n: 'Step seventeen: the answer from the start. A counter only grows, so its raw value is rarely useful. rate turns it into a per-second speed over a window, and it correctly handles counter resets when a process restarts. increase gives the total over a window instead.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'Aggregation', title: 'Aggregate with sum, avg, topk',
      cmd: ['sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', 'avg by (instance) (1 - rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m]))', 'topk(3, sum by (pod) (container_memory_working_set_bytes))', 'count(up == 0)'],
      n: 'Step eighteen: aggregation. sum by service adds up request rates per service. avg by instance gives CPU usage per machine, as one minus the idle rate. topk finds the top three memory users. And count tells you how many targets are down.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'Error rate', title: 'Ratios: the error rate',
      cmd: ['sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~"5.."}[5m]))', '  /', 'sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '0.0015    # 0.15% of requests fail'],
      tip: 'Request rate, error rate and duration are the RED method.',
      n: 'Step nineteen: ratios. Divide the rate of five hundred errors by the rate of all requests, and you get the error rate, here zero point one five percent. Together with request rate and duration, this is the RED method, a great starting point for any service.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'Percentiles', title: 'Latency percentiles from histograms',
      cmd: ['histogram_quantile(0.99,', '  sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))', '0.412     # p99 latency = 412 ms', 'histogram_quantile(0.5, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))', '0.038     # median = 38 ms'],
      tip: 'Averages hide slow requests. Alert on p95 or p99.',
      n: 'Step twenty: latency percentiles. histogram quantile calculates the ninety-ninth percentile from histogram buckets, summed by the le label, the bucket boundary. Here, ninety-nine percent of requests finish within four hundred and twelve milliseconds. Averages hide slow requests, so watch the percentiles.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'rate vs irate vs increase', cols: ['Function', 'Returns', 'Use for'],
      rows: [['rate(x[5m])', 'Per-second average over the window', 'Alerts and dashboards: smooth and stable'], ['irate(x[1m])', 'Per-second from last two samples', 'Spiky, zoomed-in graphs only'], ['increase(x[1h])', 'Total increase in the window', 'Counts: errors in the last hour'], ['delta(x[2h])', 'Change of a gauge', 'Gauges only, never counters']],
      cmd: ['rate(http_requests_total[5m])', 'increase(http_requests_total[1h])', 'rate(http_requests_total[5m] offset 1w)', 'sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m * 2]))      # duration expressions: on by default since 3.14'],
      tip: 'Make the range at least 4x the scrape interval. Never apply rate() to a gauge.',
      n: 'Step twenty one: rate, irate, and increase. Rate gives the per second average over a window, and is the right choice for alerts and dashboards. Irate uses only the last two samples, which is good for zoomed in, spiky graphs but noisy for alerts. Increase gives the total over the window. Use a range at least four times the scrape interval. Since Prometheus three point fourteen, duration arithmetic like five minutes times two is on by default.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'Vector matching: joining metrics', cmd: ['# attach team ownership to a metric', 'sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '  * on (service) group_left (team)', '    team_info', '# percentage of requests per handler', 'sum by (handler) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '  / ignoring (handler) group_left', '    sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '# a default for a missing side (needs --enable-feature=promql-binop-fill-modifiers)', 'a + fill(0) b'],
      tip: 'group_left means the left side has many series per right-side series. fill() was added in 3.10.',
      n: 'Step twenty two: joining metrics. On picks the matching labels, ignoring excludes some. Group left says the left side has many series for each series on the right, which is how you attach an owner or version label from an info metric. Since Prometheus three point ten, a fill modifier supplies a default when one side is missing, behind a feature flag.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'Aggregation, with and without', cols: ['Query', 'Result'],
      rows: [['sum by (job) (rate(x[5m]))', 'One series per job'], ['sum without (instance, pod) (rate(x[5m]))', 'Keeps every other label'], ['topk(5, sum by (handler) (rate(x[5m])))', 'The 5 busiest handlers'], ['count by (job) (up)', 'Targets per job'], ['group by (job) (up)', 'Existence, value always 1'], ['quantile(0.9, node_load1)', 'Across series, not time']],
      tip: 'Prefer "without" in recording rules when labels may be added later. "by" drops everything else.',
      n: 'Step twenty three: aggregation. Sum by keeps only the labels you name. Sum without drops the ones you name and keeps the rest, which is safer in recording rules because new labels survive. Top k finds the busiest handlers. Count counts series, and group returns presence only. Quantile aggregates across series, while quantile over time works across time.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'Over-time functions and subqueries', cmd: ['avg_over_time(node_load1[1h])', 'max_over_time(process_resident_memory_bytes[1d])', 'quantile_over_time(0.95, http_request_duration_seconds_sum[1h])', 'max_over_time(rate(http_requests_total[5m])[1h:1m])     # subquery: rate each minute, max over 1h', 'predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes[6h], 24 * 3600) < 0', 'deriv(node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes[30m])'],
      tip: 'Subqueries are expensive. Use a recording rule when a dashboard runs one constantly.',
      n: 'Step twenty four: functions over time. Average, maximum, and quantile over a window. A subquery, the square brackets with a colon, evaluates an inner expression at each step and then aggregates: here the maximum per minute rate over an hour. Predict linear extrapolates a trend, ideal for disks that will fill. Subqueries are costly, so turn frequent ones into recording rules.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'Useful alert-style queries', cols: ['Question', 'PromQL'],
      rows: [['Disk full within 24h?', 'predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes[6h], 24*3600) < 0'], ['Memory below 10%?', 'node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes < 0.1'], ['Pod restarting?', 'increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[15m]) > 3'], ['Deployment short of replicas?', 'kube_deployment_status_replicas_available / kube_deployment_spec_replicas < 1'], ['CPU throttled?', 'throttled_periods / periods (cfs)'], ['Job overdue?', 'time() - kube_job_status_completion_time > 86400']],
      tip: 'Every expression on this page was parsed with promtool 3.16 before it went into the video.',
      n: 'Step twenty five: a handful of queries worth keeping. Will the disk fill within a day? Is available memory below ten percent? Is a pod restarting more than three times in fifteen minutes? Is a deployment short of replicas? Is a container throttled by its CPU limit? Has a nightly job not completed for a day? Each one was parsed with promtool three point sixteen before it went into this video.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL deep', title: 'Find and fix slow queries', items: [['search', 'Query stats in the UI', 'Enable stats: samples, series, duration'], ['chart', 'prometheus_engine_query_duration_seconds', 'Where evaluation time goes'], ['gauge', '--query.max-samples', 'Hard cap against runaway queries'], ['refresh', 'Recording rules', 'Precompute what dashboards repeat']],
      cmd: ['topk(10, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~".+"}))     # which metric names have the most series', 'count({__name__=~".+"})                                  # total active series'],
      n: 'Step twenty six: slow queries. Turn on query statistics in the interface to see samples loaded and time spent. The engine exposes its own duration metrics, and the max samples flag caps runaway queries. The fix is usually a recording rule. And to find where your series are, count by metric name, which leads straight to cardinality problems.' },
    { sec: 'PromQL', short: 'Recording rules', title: 'Recording rules: precompute queries',
      cmd: ['groups:', '- name: api.rules', '  rules:', '  - record: service:http_requests:rate5m', '    expr: sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '  - record: service:http_errors:ratio5m', '    expr: sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total{code=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))'],
      tip: 'Naming convention: level:metric:operations.',
      n: 'Step twenty seven: recording rules precompute expensive queries on a schedule and store the result as a new series. Dashboards and alerts then load instantly. Follow the naming convention of level, metric, and operations, separated by colons.' },
    { sec: 'SLOs and alerts', title: 'Recording rules naming and structure',
      cmd: ['groups:', '  - name: shop-slo', '    interval: 30s', '    rules:', '      - record: job:http_requests:rate5m', '        expr: sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '      - record: job:http_errors:ratio_rate5m', '        expr: |', '          sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total{code=~"5.."}[5m]))', '          / sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', '$ promtool check rules rules.yml', '  SUCCESS: 8 rules found'],
      tip: 'Name rules level:metric:operations, for example job:http_errors:ratio_rate5m.',
      n: 'Step twenty eight: recording rules. Group them, set an interval, and name each result level, colon, metric, colon, operations, so everyone can read what it is. Here are the request rate and the error ratio per job. Always validate with promtool check rules; in this file it found eight valid rules.' },
    { sec: 'SLOs and alerts', title: 'SLOs and the error budget', cols: ['SLO', 'Allowed downtime / 30 days', 'Error budget'],
      rows: [['99%', '7 h 12 min', '1%'], ['99.9%', '43 min 12 s', '0.1%'], ['99.95%', '21 min 36 s', '0.05%'], ['99.99%', '4 min 19 s', '0.01%']],
      tip: 'The budget is what you may spend on risk: deploys, experiments, maintenance.',
      n: 'Step twenty nine: service level objectives. A target of ninety nine point nine percent over thirty days allows about forty three minutes of failure; that allowance is the error budget. The budget is what you can spend on risk: deployments, experiments, and maintenance. When it is nearly gone, you slow down and invest in reliability.' },
    { sec: 'SLOs and alerts', title: 'Multi-window burn-rate alerts',
      cmd: ['- alert: ErrorBudgetBurnFast', '  expr: |', '    job:http_errors:ratio_rate5m > (14.4 * 0.001)', '    and', '    sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total{code=~"5.."}[1h]))', '      / sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[1h])) > (14.4 * 0.001)', '  for: 2m', '  labels: { severity: page, team: platform }', '  annotations:', '    summary: "{{ $labels.job }} is burning its 30-day error budget 14x too fast"', '    runbook_url: https://runbooks.example.com/error-budget'],
      tip: '14.4x burn over 1h consumes 2% of a 30-day budget. Pair a fast page with a slow ticket (6x over 6h).',
      n: 'Step thirty: burn rate alerts. A burn rate of one uses the budget exactly over the period. At fourteen point four times, you would use two percent of a thirty day budget in one hour, so page. The alert requires both a short and a long window to agree, which stops flapping. Add a slower alert, around six times over six hours, as a ticket. This rule passed promtool check, and a unit test.' },
    { sec: 'SLOs and alerts', title: 'Unit-test your alerts with promtool',
      cmd: ['rule_files: [rules.yml]', 'evaluation_interval: 1m', 'tests:', '  - interval: 1m', '    input_series:', '      - { series: \'http_requests_total{job="api", code="200"}\', values: \'0+600x30\' }', '      - { series: \'http_requests_total{job="api", code="500"}\', values: \'0+60x30\' }', '    alert_rule_test:', '      - eval_time: 15m', '        alertname: ErrorBudgetBurnFast', '        exp_alerts:', '          - exp_labels: { severity: page, team: platform, job: api }', '$ promtool test rules tests.yml', 'SUCCESS'],
      tip: 'A test that fails when it should is proof it works. Run promtool test rules in CI on every change.',
      n: 'Step thirty one: test your alerts. Feed synthetic series into the rules: ten percent of requests failing is nine percent errors, far above the threshold. Assert which alert fires at fifteen minutes, with which labels. Run promtool test rules in your pipeline. I also ran this with a deliberately wrong threshold and it failed, which proves the test actually checks something.' },
    { sec: 'SLOs and alerts', title: 'Symptoms over causes: what to page on', cols: ['Page (wakes someone)', 'Ticket (next working day)', 'Dashboard only'],
      rows: [['Error budget burning fast', 'Slow burn, disk fills in 4h', 'CPU 80% on one node'], ['Public endpoint down', 'Certificate expires in 14 days', 'Memory trends'], ['Data loss or replication broken', 'Pod restarting repeatedly', 'GC pauses']],
      tip: 'Every page needs a runbook link and an owner. If nobody acts on an alert, delete it.',
      n: 'Step thirty two: what to page on. Wake people for user impact: a fast burning error budget, a public endpoint down, data loss. Create tickets for things that need fixing soon but not now: a slow burn, a disk filling in hours, a certificate expiring in two weeks. Leave causes like a hot CPU on one node to dashboards. Every page needs a runbook link and an owner, and an alert nobody acts on should be deleted.' },

    { sec: 'Alerting', short: 'Alert rules', title: 'Alerting rules',
      cmd: ['- alert: HighErrorRate', '  expr: service:http_errors:ratio5m > 0.05', '  for: 10m', '  labels: { severity: page }', '  annotations:', '    summary: "{{ $labels.service }} error rate is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }}"', '    runbook_url: https://runbooks.example.com/high-error-rate'],
      tip: 'for: 10m stops alerts firing on a single bad scrape.',
      n: 'Step thirty three: alerting rules. This one fires when a service\'s error rate stays above five percent for ten minutes. The for clause prevents alerts on a single bad scrape. Labels set the severity, and annotations add a summary and a runbook link for whoever gets paged.' },
    { sec: 'Alerting', short: 'Alertmanager', title: 'Alertmanager: route, group, silence',
      cmd: ['route:', '  receiver: slack-default', '  group_by: [alertname, service]', '  group_wait: 30s', '  routes:', '    - matchers: [severity="page"]', '      receiver: pagerduty-oncall', 'inhibit_rules:', '  - { source_matchers: [alertname="NodeDown"], target_matchers: [severity="warning"], equal: [instance] }'],
      n: 'Step thirty four: Alertmanager decides who hears about what. Group related alerts into one notification. Route pages to the on-call rotation, and everything else to chat. Inhibition hides warnings from a machine that is already down, and silences mute alerts during maintenance.' },
    { sec: 'Alertmanager deep', title: 'A production routing tree',
      cmd: ['route:', '  receiver: slack-default', '  group_by: [alertname, cluster, service]', '  group_wait: 30s', '  group_interval: 5m', '  repeat_interval: 4h', '  routes:', '    - matchers: [severity="page"]', '      receiver: pagerduty', '    - matchers: [team="data"]', '      receiver: slack-data', '      mute_time_intervals: [out-of-hours]', '$ amtool check-config alertmanager.yml', 'Checking \'alertmanager.yml\'  SUCCESS'],
      tip: 'First matching route wins unless continue: true. Put the most specific routes first.',
      n: 'Step thirty five: a routing tree. Alerts are grouped by name, cluster, and service, so one outage sends one notification. Group wait collects related alerts for thirty seconds, group interval batches new ones, and repeat interval re sends every four hours. Pages go to PagerDuty, data team alerts go to their channel but are muted out of hours. The first matching route wins, unless you set continue. amtool check config validated this file.' },
    { sec: 'Alertmanager deep', title: 'Test routing before an incident',
      cmd: ['$ amtool config routes test --config.file=alertmanager.yml severity=page alertname=HighErrors', 'pagerduty', '$ amtool config routes test --config.file=alertmanager.yml team=data severity=ticket', 'slack-data', '$ amtool config routes test --config.file=alertmanager.yml severity=ticket', 'slack-default', '$ amtool config routes show --config.file=alertmanager.yml'],
      tip: 'These three results are real output from amtool 0.34.2 against the config on the previous slide.',
      n: 'Step thirty six: test routing. Amtool config routes test tells you which receiver an alert with given labels would reach. A page goes to PagerDuty. A data team ticket goes to the data channel. Anything else goes to the default Slack channel. These three results are real output from amtool zero point thirty four against the previous configuration. Add this test to your pipeline.' },
    { sec: 'Alertmanager deep', title: 'Inhibition, silences and time intervals',
      cmd: ['inhibit_rules:', '  - source_matchers: [alertname="InstanceDown"]', '    target_matchers: [severity="ticket"]', '    equal: [instance]', 'time_intervals:', '  - name: out-of-hours', '    time_intervals:', '      - weekdays: ["saturday", "sunday"]', '      - times: [{ start_time: "18:00", end_time: "24:00" }]', '$ amtool silence add alertname=DiskWillFillIn4h instance=db1:9100 --duration=2h --comment="planned resize" --author=kona', '$ amtool silence query'],
      tip: 'Inhibition: when a host is down, suppress its dependent alerts. Silences expire; always add a comment.',
      n: 'Step thirty seven: reducing noise. An inhibition rule suppresses ticket level alerts for an instance while it is already down, so you get one alert, not thirty. Time intervals mute routes at weekends or after hours. A silence mutes a known issue for a limited time: always give a duration, a comment, and your name, so the next engineer understands.' },
    { sec: 'Alertmanager deep', title: 'Receivers, templates and high availability', items: [['bell', 'Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie, email, webhook', 'Use *_file secrets, not inline keys'], ['file', 'Notification templates', 'Link to dashboard, runbook, and query'], ['layers', 'HA cluster of 3', 'Gossip deduplicates; point every Prometheus at all of them'], ['eye', 'Dead man\'s switch', 'An always-firing alert proves the pipeline works']],
      n: 'Step thirty eight: receivers and resilience. Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie, email, and webhooks, with secrets read from files rather than written inline. Templates can add dashboard and runbook links. Run Alertmanager as a cluster of three; they gossip to deduplicate, and every Prometheus should send to all of them. And add a dead man switch: an alert that always fires, so you are paged if the whole alerting pipeline stops.' },
    { sec: 'Alerting', short: 'Good alerts', title: 'What makes a good alert',
      items: [['user', 'Alert on symptoms users feel', 'Errors, latency, availability'], ['gauge', 'Use SLO burn-rate alerts', 'Fast and slow windows'], ['book', 'Every alert has a runbook', 'What to check, how to fix'], ['x', 'Delete alerts nobody acts on', 'Noise causes missed pages']],
      n: 'Step thirty nine: good alerts. Alert on symptoms users feel, like errors and latency, not on every CPU spike. Burn rate alerts on your service level objectives catch both fast and slow problems. Give every alert a runbook. And delete alerts nobody acts on, because noise makes people miss real pages.' },

    { sec: 'Kubernetes', short: 'kube-prometheus-stack', title: 'Monitor Kubernetes in one install',
      cmd: ['$ helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts', '$ helm install mon prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack -n monitoring --create-namespace', '$ kubectl get pods -n monitoring', 'prometheus-mon-kube-prometheus-stack-prometheus-0   2/2   Running', 'alertmanager-mon-kube-prometheus-stack-alertmanager-0   2/2   Running', 'mon-grafana-6f8d9b7c5-x2kfp   3/3   Running'],
      n: 'Step forty: on Kubernetes, the kube prometheus stack chart installs everything at once: the Prometheus Operator, Prometheus, Alertmanager, node exporter, kube state metrics, and Grafana with ready-made dashboards and alerts.' },
    { sec: 'Kubernetes', short: 'ServiceMonitor', title: 'Scrape your app: ServiceMonitor',
      cmd: ['apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1', 'kind: ServiceMonitor', 'metadata: { name: api, labels: { release: mon } }', 'spec:', '  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }', '  endpoints: [{ port: metrics, interval: 30s }]'],
      tip: 'The release label must match what your Prometheus selects.',
      n: 'Step forty one: with the operator, you add scrape targets as Kubernetes objects. A ServiceMonitor selects your app\'s service by label, and names the metrics port. The release label must match the one your Prometheus is configured to select.' },
    { sec: 'Kubernetes deep', title: 'The Prometheus Operator objects', cols: ['Object', 'Tells the operator to…'],
      rows: [['Prometheus', 'Run a Prometheus server (StatefulSet)'], ['ServiceMonitor', 'Scrape the pods behind a Service'], ['PodMonitor', 'Scrape pods directly (no Service)'], ['Probe', 'Probe URLs with the blackbox exporter'], ['PrometheusRule', 'Load recording and alerting rules'], ['AlertmanagerConfig', 'Route alerts from a namespace'], ['ScrapeConfig', 'Scrape anything else']],
      tip: 'Match labels: the Prometheus selects monitors by a label such as release=kube-prometheus-stack.',
      n: 'Step forty two: the Operator objects. A Prometheus object runs a server. Service monitors scrape the pods behind a service, pod monitors scrape pods directly, and probes check U R Ls. Prometheus rule objects load rules. Alertmanager config routes alerts per namespace. And scrape config covers anything else. The server picks up monitors by label selectors, so the label on your object must match.' },
    { sec: 'Kubernetes deep', title: 'ServiceMonitor and PrometheusRule YAML',
      cmd: ['apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1', 'kind: ServiceMonitor', 'metadata: { name: shop-api, namespace: shop, labels: { release: kube-prometheus-stack } }', 'spec:', '  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }', '  namespaceSelector: { matchNames: [shop] }', '  endpoints:', '    - { port: http, path: /metrics, interval: 30s, scrapeTimeout: 10s }', '---', 'kind: PrometheusRule', 'metadata: { name: shop-slo, labels: { release: kube-prometheus-stack } }', 'spec: { groups: [ … the same rules as before … ] }'],
      tip: 'These manifests were validated against the Operator CRD schemas with kubeconform.',
      n: 'Step forty three: the manifests. A service monitor selects the service by label, in the shop namespace, and scrapes the http port every thirty seconds. A Prometheus rule object carries the same alert rules as before, so your unit tests still apply. Both carry the release label the Prometheus server selects on. I validated these manifests against the Operator schemas with kubeconform.' },
    { sec: 'Kubernetes deep', title: 'Install the whole stack with Helm',
      cmd: ['$ helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts', '$ helm repo update', '$ helm install kps prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack -n monitoring --create-namespace \\', '    --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.retention=15d \\', '    --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.serviceMonitorSelectorNilUsesHelmValues=false', '$ kubectl get pods -n monitoring', '$ kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/kps-kube-prometheus-stack-prometheus 9090'],
      tip: 'serviceMonitorSelectorNilUsesHelmValues=false makes Prometheus pick up monitors from every namespace.',
      n: 'Step forty four: install the full stack. The kube Prometheus stack chart installs the Operator, Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, node exporter, kube state metrics, and a large set of ready made alerts and dashboards. Setting the selector value to false makes Prometheus pick up monitors from every namespace, instead of only those from the chart. Port forward to look at it.' },
    { sec: 'Kubernetes deep', title: 'Kubernetes metrics worth knowing', cols: ['Source', 'Examples', 'Tells you'],
      rows: [['kube-state-metrics', 'kube_pod_status_phase, …replicas_available', 'Desired vs actual object state'], ['cAdvisor (kubelet)', 'container_cpu_usage…, …working_set_bytes', 'Real resource use'], ['node-exporter', 'node_cpu_seconds…, …filesystem_avail', 'Node health'], ['API server', 'apiserver_request_duration_seconds', 'Control plane latency'], ['Your app', 'http_requests_total', 'User experience']],
      tip: 'Compare usage with requests to right-size pods: usage / requests, per container.',
      n: 'Step forty five: the four metric sources on Kubernetes. Kube state metrics reports desired versus actual state of objects. C Advisor, through the kubelet, reports real container CPU and memory. Node exporter covers the nodes. The A P I server exposes control plane latency. And your app is the one that matters most. Compare usage with requests to right size your pods.' },

    { sec: 'Scale', short: 'Cardinality', title: 'Cardinality: the silent killer',
      items: [['warn', 'Each label combination = a series', 'Series use memory'], ['x', 'Never label with user IDs or URLs', 'Unbounded values explode'], ['search', 'Find the worst offenders', 'topk(10, count by (__name__)({__name__=~".+"}))'], ['check', 'Keep labels bounded', 'method, code, service']],
      n: 'Step forty six: cardinality. Every unique combination of label values is a separate series, and each one uses memory. Never put user IDs, email addresses, or full URLs in labels, because their values are unbounded. Keep labels to small, fixed sets, like method and status code.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'Find and cut high cardinality', cmd: ['topk(10, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~".+"}))', 'topk(10, count by (job) ({__name__=~".+"}))', 'sum(scrape_samples_scraped) by (job)', 'prometheus_tsdb_head_series', '# prevent a runaway target from taking Prometheus down:', 'scrape_configs:', '  - job_name: app', '    sample_limit: 50000', '    label_limit: 30', '$ promtool tsdb analyze /prometheus'],
      tip: 'sample_limit fails the scrape instead of exploding memory. promtool tsdb analyze names the worst labels.',
      n: 'Step forty seven: find and cut cardinality. Count series by metric name and by job to find the offenders. Watch the head series metric. Protect Prometheus with a sample limit and a label limit per scrape: a misbehaving target then fails its scrape instead of exhausting memory. And promtool tsdb analyze lists the metrics and labels with the most unique values.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'Remote write, and where long-term data lives', cols: ['Option', 'Model', 'Good for'],
      rows: [['Thanos', 'Sidecar or receive + object storage', 'Global view of many Prometheus servers'], ['Grafana Mimir', 'Horizontally scalable remote-write store', 'Multi-tenant, very large scale'], ['Cortex / VictoriaMetrics', 'Remote-write stores', 'Cost-efficient long retention'], ['Managed: AMP, Azure Monitor, GMP', 'Cloud-hosted', 'No servers to run']],
      cmd: ['remote_write:', '  - url: https://mimir.example.com/api/v1/push', '    queue_config: { max_samples_per_send: 5000 }'],
      tip: 'Remote Write 2.0 sends metadata and native histograms efficiently. Keep local retention short (15 days).',
      n: 'Step forty eight: long term storage. Prometheus itself is a single node database with local storage. For a global view and long retention, send data on with remote write to Thanos, Grafana Mimir, Cortex, VictoriaMetrics, or a managed service from your cloud. Remote Write two point zero carries metadata and native histograms efficiently. Keep local retention short, around fifteen days.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'High availability and sharding', items: [['layers', 'HA pair', 'Two identical servers, same config, external label replica=0/1'], ['filter', 'Deduplicate at query time', 'Thanos Query or Mimir dedupe on the replica label'], ['server', 'Functional sharding', 'One Prometheus per team, region, or workload'], ['gear', 'Agent mode', 'Scrape and forward only; no local storage']],
      cmd: ['global:', '  external_labels: { cluster: prod-eu-west-1, replica: "0" }', '$ prometheus --agent --config.file=agent.yml          # forward-only mode'],
      n: 'Step forty nine: availability and scale. Run an identical pair, distinguished by a replica external label, and deduplicate when querying with Thanos or Mimir. Shard by team, region, or workload when one server is too big. And agent mode runs Prometheus as a lightweight scraper that forwards to remote storage with no local database, ideal on edge clusters.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'OpenTelemetry and Prometheus together', items: [['cloud', 'OTLP receiver', 'Prometheus accepts OpenTelemetry metrics natively'], ['filter', 'Resource attributes → labels', 'promote_all_resource_attributes, ignore_resource_attributes'], ['star', 'UTF-8 metric names (3.0)', 'Dots in OTel names kept as-is'], ['bell', 'Alloy / OTel Collector', 'Receive, process and forward']],
      tip: 'Choose one pipeline for metrics. Mixing pull and push is fine, but document who owns which.',
      n: 'Step fifty: OpenTelemetry. Prometheus can receive OTLP metrics directly, with options to promote resource attributes to labels or ignore them. Since Prometheus three, UTF eight metric and label names are supported, so the dots in OpenTelemetry names no longer need translating. Grafana Alloy or the OpenTelemetry Collector can receive, process, and forward telemetry. Whichever pipeline you choose, document who owns what.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'What changed from Prometheus 3.0 to 3.16', cols: ['Version', 'Date', 'Highlights'],
      rows: [['3.0', 'Nov 2024', 'New UI, UTF-8 names, OTLP, RW 2.0'], ['3.4', 'May 2025', 'OTLP delta metrics, arithmetic in durations'], ['3.7', 'Oct 2025', 'Extended range selectors (anchored, smoothed)'], ['3.9', 'Jan 2026', 'Native histograms stable; /api/v1/features'], ['3.10', 'Feb 2026', 'fill() binary-operator modifiers, AWS MSK SD'], ['3.14', 'Aug 2026', 'Duration expressions on by default'], ['3.15', 'Sep 2026', 'Unix socket scrapes, runtime.log_level in config'], ['3.16', 'current', 'Latest release at the time of recording']],
      tip: 'Read each release\'s [CHANGE] entries before upgrading. 3.0 changed scrape fallbacks and le normalisation.',
      n: 'Step fifty one: what changed. Prometheus three point zero brought the new interface, UTF eight names, OTLP ingestion, and Remote Write two. Three point four added duration arithmetic. Three point nine made native histograms stable. Three point ten added fill modifiers. Three point fourteen turned duration expressions on by default, and three point fifteen added scraping over Unix sockets and a log level in the configuration. Three point sixteen is the current release. Read the change entries before every upgrade.' },
    { sec: 'Scale deep', title: 'Prometheus checklist', items: [['gauge', 'RED/USE metrics, bounded labels, seconds and bytes', 'promtool check metrics'], ['search', 'Recording rules with level:metric:operation names', 'Check rules in CI'], ['bell', 'Page on SLO burn rate; ticket on slow burn', 'Unit-test every alert'], ['route', 'Tested Alertmanager routes, inhibition, dead man switch', 'amtool config routes test'], ['server', 'Sample limits, retention, remote write, HA pair', 'Watch head series']],
      n: 'Step fifty two: the checklist to take away. Metrics with the R E D and U S E methods, bounded labels, base units. Recording rules with clear names, checked in C I. Page on burn rate and unit test every alert. Tested Alertmanager routes with inhibition and a dead man switch. And sample limits, short local retention, remote write, and a high availability pair, while watching head series.' },
    { sec: 'Scale', short: 'Long-term storage', title: 'Long-term storage and high availability',
      items: [['database', 'Local TSDB: weeks of data', 'Set --storage.tsdb.retention.time'], ['layers', 'Thanos or Cortex', 'Global view + object storage'], ['zap', 'Grafana Mimir, VictoriaMetrics', 'Scalable long-term storage'], ['refresh', 'Run two Prometheus replicas', 'Alertmanager deduplicates']],
      n: 'Step fifty three: scaling up. Prometheus stores weeks of data locally, set by its retention time. For a global view and long-term storage in object storage, use Thanos, Cortex, Grafana Mimir, or VictoriaMetrics. For high availability, run two identical Prometheus servers, and Alertmanager removes duplicate alerts.' },

    { type: 'mistakes', rows: [['Graphing a raw counter', 'Wrap it in rate() or increase()'], ['Alerting on averages', 'Alert on p95/p99 and error ratios'], ['High-cardinality labels', 'No IDs, emails or full URLs'], ['Alerts without for: or runbooks', 'Add for:, severity and a runbook link']],
      n: 'Four common mistakes. Graphing a raw counter instead of its rate. Alerting on averages, which hide slow requests. Using high cardinality labels. And alerts without a for clause or a runbook, which wake people up for nothing.' },
    { type: 'repos', repos: [['prometheus/prometheus', 'Prometheus server', '', 'chart'], ['prometheus/node_exporter', 'Machine metrics exporter', '', 'server'], ['prometheus/alertmanager', 'Alert routing and silencing', '', 'bell'], ['prometheus-community/helm-charts', 'kube-prometheus-stack and more', '', 'wheel'], ['samber/awesome-prometheus-alerts', 'Hundreds of ready alert rules', '', 'warn'], ['thanos-io/thanos', 'Global view and long-term storage', '', 'layers']],
      n: 'Practice repos: Prometheus itself, node exporter, Alertmanager, the community Helm charts, awesome Prometheus alerts with hundreds of ready rules, and Thanos. Screenshot this frame.' },
    { type: 'recap', points: ['Scrape → TSDB → rules; plan memory for active series', 'Design metrics: base units, bounded labels, native histograms', 'PromQL: rate for alerts, vector matching, aggregation, subqueries', 'SLO burn-rate alerts; unit-test with promtool; test routes with amtool', 'Operator: ServiceMonitor, PodMonitor, PrometheusRule, kube-prometheus-stack', 'Scale: cardinality limits, remote write, HA, OTLP; Prometheus 3.0 to 3.16'],
      n: 'Recap. Prometheus pulls metrics and stores labelled time series. Counters only grow, which is why you wrap them in rate. Use sum by, ratios, and histogram quantile for most questions. Alert on symptoms, route with Alertmanager, and keep labels bounded. Follow for more, and comment PROMQL if this helped.' },
  ],
};
